Content-Type: text/plain


Attached is the .envrc file I previously mentioned (for Direnv).

Should be easier to maintain than a Flake, but still provide the same
feature of deploying a shell with development dependencies.

I discovered "use flake" could be used without a Flake while trying to
figure out what would be needed for Nix.
"use flake nixpkgs#emacs" seems to pull in the build dependencies,
as I was able to build Emacs with gcc (which I don't have installed in my system).

A conditional is used to check for Guix first so a conflicting shell
isn't deployed for those who have both on their system.


--=-=-=
Content-Type: text/plain
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=.envrc
Content-Description: Direnv file

# Direnv is a tool for defining development environments for projects.
# It is used to deploy a development shell using either GNU Guix or Nix.

# Check for Guix first.
if type guix; then
    # Emacs Next is an Emacs package closer to the version in development.
    use guix --development emacs-next
else
    use flake nixpkgs#emacs
fi

